artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider the following model of computation (the integer addition machine). A machine has a fixed finite number of registers, each holding an arbitrary integer. The primitive instructions, each costing one unit of time, are:
(i) add the contents of one register to another, or subtract one register's contents from another's, storing the result in a designated register;
(ii) compare the contents of two registers and branch conditionally on the outcome (=, <, >);
(iii) unconditional jump;
(iv) read an integer from input into a register;
(v) write the contents of a register to output.
No instruction loads an arbitrary integer constant into a register; the only constants directly available are those produced by the operations above (so, e.g., 0 may be obtained by subtracting a register from itself).

Question A. Does there exist an integer addition machine that uses only five registers and, on input an integer x, outputs x^2 using O(log |x|) primitive operations?

Question B. Does there exist an integer addition machine that uses only five registers and, on inputs integers y, z (with z ≠ 0 and the quotient well-defined), outputs the integer quotient ⌊y/z⌋ using O(log(y/z)) primitive operations?

